.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH PSTAT 1M
.SH NAME
pstat \- print system facts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pstat
[
.B \-acfimpstx
] [
.B \-u
addr ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pstat
interprets the contents of certain system tables.
.PP
.B Note:
every table it prints is read by name and by value through
.IR kctl (2),
which is unprivileged, so all of it works for anybody \- except
.BR \-u ,
which opens a memory device and is the super-user's.
v7 read every table off
.I /dev/mem
with a namelist taken from
.IR /unix ;
there is no kernel image on this filesystem, and the
.I file
and
.I namelist
arguments are gone with it.
.PP
.B LOC
throughout is the
.B word
address of the table entry, printed in octal, where v7 printed a
24-bit byte address in hex.
So is every
.BR IPTR ,
.BR INODP ,
.B LINK
and
.BR TEXTP :
the tables cross-reference by eye.
.PP
Options are
.TP \w'WCHAN\ 'u
.B \-a
Under
.BR \-p ,
describe all process slots rather than just active ones.
.TP
.B \-i
Print the inode table with the these headings:
.IP LOC
The core location of this table entry.
.PD 0
.IP FLAGS
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP L
locked
.IP U
update time
.IR filsys (5))
must be corrected
.IP A
access time must be corrected
.IP M
file system is mounted here
.IP W
wanted by another process (L flag is on)
.IP T
contains a text file
.IP C
changed time must be corrected
.RE
.IP CNT
Number of open file table entries for this inode.
.IP DEV
Major and minor device number of file system in which
this inode resides.
.IP INO
I-number within the device.
.IP MODE
Mode bits, see
.IR chmod (2).
.IP NLK
Number of links to this inode.
.IP UID
User ID of owner.
.IP SIZ/DEV
Number of bytes in an ordinary file, or
major and minor device of special file.
.PD
.TP
.B \-x
Print the text table with these headings:
.IP LOC
The core location of this table entry.
.PD 0
.IP FLAGS
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP T
.IR ptrace (2)
in effect
.IP W
text not yet written on swap device
.IP L
loading in progress
.IP K
locked
.IP w
wanted (L flag is on)
.RE
.PD
.IP DADDR
Disk address in swap, measured in multiples of 512 bytes.
.IP CADDR
Core address, measured in multiples of 64 bytes.
.IP SIZE
Size of text segment, measured in multiples of 64 bytes.
.IP IPTR
Core location of corresponding inode.
.IP CNT
Number of processes using this text segment.
.IP CCNT
Number of processes in core using this text segment.
.PD
.TP
.B \-p
Print process table for active processes with these headings:
.IP LOC
The core location of this table entry.
.PD 0
.IP S
Run state encoded thus:
.RS
.IP 0
no process
.IP 1
waiting for some event
.IP 3
runnable
.IP 4
being created
.IP 5
being terminated
.IP 6
stopped under trace
.RE
.IP F
Miscellaneous state variables, or-ed together, in octal:
.RS
.IP 01
loaded
.IP 02
the scheduler process
.IP 04
locked
.IP 010
swapped out
.IP 020
traced
.IP 040
used in tracing
.IP 0100
locked in by
.IR lock (2).
.RE
.IP PRI
Scheduling priority, see
.IR nice (2).
.IP SIGNAL 10
Signals received (signals 1-16 coded in bits 0-15),
.IP UID
Real user ID.
.IP TIM
Time resident in seconds; times over 127 coded as 127.
.IP CPU
Weighted integral of CPU time, for scheduler.
.IP NI
Nice level,
see
.IR nice (2).
.IP PGRP
Process number of root of process group
(the opener of the controlling terminal).
.IP PID
The process ID number.
.IP PPID
The process ID of parent process.
.IP ADDR
If in core, the physical
.B word
address of the process image, whose first
.B USIZE
words are its `u-area'.
If swapped out, its block on the paging store.
.B Note:
v7 counted 64-byte clicks and 512-byte blocks.
.IP SIZE
Size of the process image in
.BR words ,
a multiple of
.BR PGSZ .
.IP WCHAN
Wait channel number of a waiting process.
.IP LINK
Link pointer in list of runnable processes.
.IP TEXTP
If text is pure, pointer to location of text table entry.
.IP CLKT
Countdown for
.IR alarm (2)
measured in seconds.
.PD
.TP
.B \-t
Print the table for the
.B NSC
Consul typewriters, with these headings.
.B Note:
v7's handled a DL11 and up to 48 DH11 lines; this machine has one terminal
driver and one array,
.BR sc[] ,
and the row number is its subscript, which is also the device's minor number.
.IP RAW
Number of characters in raw input queue.
.PD 0
.IP CAN
Number of characters in canonicalized input queue.
.IP OUT
Number of characters in putput queue.
.IP FLAGS
See
.IR tty (4).
.B Note:
v7 called this column MODE.
.IP LINE
The device/line number.
.B Note:
v7 called this ADDR and it was the UNIBUS address of the line's registers.
This machine has no I/O address space at all \- a device is named by the
register number an
.B 033
instruction addresses \- so
.B t_addr
is a line number here and the column is renamed to say so.
.IP DEL
Number of delimiters (newlines) in canonicalized input queue.
.IP COL
Calculated column position of terminal.
.IP STATE
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP T
timeout pending.
.B Note:
nothing sets this bit now; it is kept for v7's numbering and for this program.
.IP W
waiting for open to complete
.IP O
open
.IP C
carrier is on
.IP B
busy doing output
.IP A
process is awaiting output
.IP X
open for exclusive use
.IP H
hangup on close
.RE
.B Note:
.B XCLUDE
and
.B HUPCLS
are set by
.IR ioctl (2)
and tested by nothing in this kernel, so those two letters record a request
rather than a state.
.IP PGRP
Process group for which this is controlling terminal.
.PD
.TP
.B \-u
Print information about a user process;
the next argument is its address as given
by
.IR ps (1),
in octal, and with no conversion \- both are word addresses.
An address of
.B 0
names the u-area that is live at
.BR UBASE ,
which is the caller's own.
.B Note:
if the address given is the one the live u-area belongs to, the live copy is
read instead of the process's image, the image being stale until the kernel
next flushes it.
.IR ps (1)
follows the same rule and its source is the account of it.
The process must be in main memory: a swapped-out u-area is on the paging
store and this program does not read it.
This is the only option that opens a device, and so the only one that needs
to be root.
.TP
.B \-f
Print the open file table with these headings:
.IP LOC
The core location of this table entry.
.PD 0
.IP FLG
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP R
open for reading
.IP W
open for writing
.IP P
pipe
.RE
.IP CNT
Number of processes that know this open file.
.IP INO
The location of the inode table entry for this file.
.IP OFFS
The file offset, see
.IR lseek (2).
.PD
.TP
.B \-m
.B Note:
.B this option is new.
Print the mount table \- one line per mounted file system \- with the headings
LOC, DEVICE, BUFP (the buffer holding its super-block) and INODP (the inode it
is mounted on).
Slot 0 is the root and is always in use.
With
.B \-a
the empty slots are printed too.
.TP
.B \-s
.B Note:
.B this option is new.
Print the paging store and the two allocation maps:
the swap device and its
.B swplo
and
.BR nswap ,
then
.B coremap
and
.BR swapmap ,
each with its free fragments and their total.
.B The units differ:
.B coremap
hands out
.B words
of physical core and
.B swapmap
hands out
.B blocks
of
.BR BSIZE .
.TP
.B \-c
.B Note:
.B this option is new.
Print the system clock:
.B time
in seconds since the epoch, and
.B lbolt
as a fraction of
.B HZ
into the current second, followed by the date.
.IR time (2)
returns whole seconds, so the fraction is visible nowhere else.
.PP
.B Note:
the three options above exist because the kernel exports the tables behind them
and nothing else read them.
A variable in
.BR kernel/ksym.c 's
table names the program that asked for it, and
.BR mount ,
.BR coremap ,
.BR swapmap ,
.BR nswap ,
.BR swplo ,
.BR swapdev ,
.B time
and
.B lbolt
all named this one.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev/kmem  'u
/dev/kmem	the live u-area, for
.B \-u
.br
/dev/mem	any other resident u-area, for
.B \-u
.PP
Everything else comes through
.IR kctl (2)
and opens nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
kctl(2), ps(1), iostat(1M), dmesg(1), stat(2), filsys(5)
.br
K. Thompson,
.I UNIX Implementation
